{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall put on his linen garment, etc.</b> The Ramban explained that the removal of the ashes requires the priestly garments, and there is no divine service performed in two garments; nevertheless, Scripture specifies these particular garments on account of a novelty that is renewed through them: that the tunic should be to his measure, and that nothing should intervene between the breeches and his flesh. And the question arises on this: why did it specify these novelties here? It should have written them in parashat תְּצַוֶּה, for there is the place where it speaks of the arrangement of the garments." "And I say to resolve this: since the olah atones for the straying of the heart, whose principal time is at night, and the principal straying is in forbidden relations — for from this straying a person may come to an actual act on his own, namely the impurity of emission, and this matter is also called 'shedding of blood,' for the emission of seed in vain that comes through straying is like shedding blood, that is, the blood of his seed, as they learned from the verse ״שׁוֹחֲטֵי הַיְלָדִים בַּנְּחָלִים״ (Isaiah 57:5) — read not שׁוֹחֲטֵי but סוֹחֲטֵי (Niddah 13a). And you already know that the breeches atone for forbidden relations and the tunic atones for shedding of blood, as is found in tractate Arachin (16a), and the olah atones for the straying that encompasses forbidden relations and shedding of blood together. Therefore, regarding the removal of the ashes of the olah, it is stated ״וְלָבַשׁ הַכֹּהֵן מִדּוֹ בַד״ — this is the tunic that atones for shedding of blood; ״וּמִכְנְסֵי בַד״ — the breeches that atone for forbidden relations. And therefore it specifies ״אֲשֶׁר תֹּאכַל הָאֵשׁ אֶת הָעֹלָה״, to tell you that even though there is in the ash a mixture of other species, nevertheless this removal is not in these two garments except because the ash of the olah is in it, which atones for the straying of the heart. And since in truth there is here neither actual forbidden relations nor actual shedding of blood but only something that resembles them, you might suppose that even if the tunic were not to his measure and even if something intervened between the breeches and his flesh, it would not be a disqualification — therefore it specifies to say that here too they must be in their proper arrangement from every side." "And in the Midrash (Leviticus Rabbah 7:6) they said: ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת הָעֹלָה הִוא הָעֹלָה עַל מוֹקְדָה״ — whoever is arrogant is judged by fire, etc. His intention is not to remove this verse from its plain sense and to interpret ״הִוא הָעֹלָה״ as referring to the arrogant person, but rather the intent of this Midrash is to say that the olah atones for haughtiness of spirit. And it has some support from the verse ״וְהָעוֹלָה עַל רוּחֲכֶם״ (Ezekiel 20:32), that is, upon the haughtiness of spirit (רוּחַ); therefore the law of the olah is in fire, whose nature is to rise upward. Therefore it says ״הִוא הָעֹלָה עַל מוֹקְדָהּ״, for the flame of the altar's fire that rises upward is an atonement for the haughty spirit that rises in grandeur and wonders beyond him." "And with this intent the removal of the ashes was mentioned, for the remedy is the ash: that one should say ״וְאָנֹכִי עָפָר וָאֵפֶר״ like Abraham, and then he shall be raised and lifted up and exalted very high, for whoever humbles himself — the Holy One, blessed be He, raises him up; and this is what it says: ״וְהֵרִים אֶת הַדֶּשֶׁן״. And for this reason it specified these two garments that the atoning priest shall wear: ״וְלָבַשׁ הַכֹּהֵן מִדּוֹ בַד״ — to his measure, that the garment of his dignity should not be longer than him but to his measure, for 'whoever wears a garment shall wear it to his measure' (Berakhot 28a) — to his measure, and he shall not walk in grandeur and wonders beyond him; ״וּמִכְנְסֵי בַד״ — which atone for forbidden relations, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (see Sotah 4a): whoever is arrogant is as though he comes upon all the forbidden relations, etc.; and the placing of the ash beside the altar — for the altar too points to the boundary of humility, as explained above in parashat Mishpatim." "And some say: since the removal of the ashes is light and contemptible labor, there is reason to be concerned that perhaps the priest would put on short garments so they would not get soiled in the ash, or would wear something intervening against his flesh so as not to soil his flesh with the ashes — therefore it stated that the tunic should be to his measure and that nothing should intervene between him and the breeches."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:30.465801241Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}